WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.880
My name is Derrick
Winje from The Cobblers.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.870
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.660
how me and my team restored the midsole

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.890
of this Bred 2012 Jordan 4.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.590
The shoe we're working
on today specifically

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:16.823
is the Bred 12,

00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:18.970
which is a nickname for black and red,

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.287
which is very common in
what you find in Jordans.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.020
The value of the shoe in the
condition that it came in

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.130
is probably around $200 to $225.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.462
If you're lucky enough to find a pair

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.580
that no one has worn
in the last nine years,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.263
you're looking at about $550

00:00:33.263 --> 00:00:34.154
for the value of the shoe

00:00:34.154 --> 00:00:36.497
if you have all the original
accessories and the box.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.580
We're going to be removing all the paint

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.690
from the midsole to prep
the midsole for new paint.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.598
We're then going to lay down

00:00:43.598 --> 00:00:44.764
some base layers of white

00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:47.070
and finish it up with a solid white coat.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.560
Then we're going to go
ahead and tape up that white

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.220
to add our second color, which is black,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.512
so it looks original.

00:00:55.663 --> 00:00:56.760
This shoe is nine years old,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.018
which is another reason why

00:00:58.018 --> 00:00:59.117
this shoe needs to be repainted,

00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.320
because it's been worn a little bit

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.510
and the paint's starting to crack.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.110
The most difficult part
of this restoration

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.435
is keeping the midsoles looking

00:01:05.435 --> 00:01:07.593
smooth and factory-clean.

00:01:07.593 --> 00:01:09.720
The issue that we have as
these shoes start to age

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.920
is that the EVA foam,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.760
which is what the midsole is comprised of,

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.950
starts to break down.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.170
It's our job to try and
keep that looking original

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.710
and smooth by removing the paint safely,

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.330
depending on the type of
shoe, the type of paint,

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.880
as well as the age of the shoe.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.470
The paint on the midsole
is starting to chip

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.480
on this shoe because it's from 2012.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.313
So we're going to go ahead and

00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:30.060
remove all this paint to get started.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.450
We're actually using a dulled blade

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.960
to scrape away this paint carefully.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.760
Depending on the year
and the model of shoe,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.400
they use different types of paints.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.020
On these 2012 Bred 4s,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.190
it's actually an acrylic paint.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.200
You can tell because
it's a little bit flaky,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.380
and that's why we're
going to be chipping away,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.650
as opposed to using a solvent

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.008
or other type of chemical to remove that.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.088
It's necessary to remove the old paint

00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:54.270
so we can get a nice clean
surface to adhere to.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.720
This allows the paint
to be flexible, durable,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.920
and to give us the
smoothest finish possible.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.700
When removing the paint with this method,

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.740
we really want to focus
on scraping just the paint

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.638
and not digging down into
that EVA foam midsole.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.770
So, once we have the majority,

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.060
if not all of the paint
removed from scraping,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:12.970
I'm going to go ahead and go back

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.230
with some denatured alcohol,

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.140
or isopropyl alcohol works as well,

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.290
to just wipe down the surface

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.140
to make sure I got any debris or oil

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.140
that might've built up on that midsole.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.220
So, once we've removed
all that original paint

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.503
and cleaned it with alcohol,

00:02:25.503 --> 00:02:26.336
we're going to go ahead and start

00:02:26.336 --> 00:02:27.890
with our base coats of white.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.480
Before adding that paint,

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.860
I'm going to go ahead
and tape up these areas

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.500
to make sure I don't
get paint on any areas

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.940
that I don't want it.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.123
The trick here for taping off

00:02:38.123 --> 00:02:39.573
is just to take your time

00:02:39.573 --> 00:02:40.485
and really make sure

00:02:40.485 --> 00:02:42.043
you're putting your
tape where you need it.

00:02:42.043 --> 00:02:42.880
Once you've applied it,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.700
I always like to take a
step back and look at it

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.050
to make sure I have a second eye,

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.650
or a second angle to make
sure I did that accurately.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.460
Almost every time we're
repainting midsoles,

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.190
I'm going to use a base layer of paint,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.920
which is actually just the color I'm using

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.140
when I'm first painting.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.488
In this case it's going to be white

00:02:58.488 --> 00:02:59.917
before I do the black, so I'm going to do

00:02:59.917 --> 00:03:01.890
some light coats of
white to build up a base

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.415
and then do more coats of white

00:03:03.415 --> 00:03:05.400
to get a full, solid color.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.950
When I'm applying the white
paint as the base layer,

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.800
I make sure I apply that
all over for a few coats,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.580
but once I have about two
coats down as a base coat,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:14.920
I'll then focus on the area where

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.430
the white paint is actually visible

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.370
once we're going to be
done with the paint job.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.800
In this case, that's
going to be on the bottom

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.970
of the midsole, as the
top is going to be black.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.640
So, once we finish the white paint,

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.020
you can see it's a nice,
solid color on the bottom.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.195
And now we need to do the top,

00:03:28.195 --> 00:03:29.311
which is going to be black.

00:03:29.311 --> 00:03:30.197
For this, I'm going to be using

00:03:30.197 --> 00:03:31.860
a quarter-inch blue painter's tape

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.890
to tape off the bottom,

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:35.220
so I can put the black
paint on top cleanly.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.100
You can use any tape you really want.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.134
You just need to make sure you can get

00:03:38.134 --> 00:03:39.591
a clean, crisp edge.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.850
When we're applying this
tape for the second color,

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.910
we need it to be very precise.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.040
On this particular shoe,
there is a little ledge here

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.880
that I can tuck the tape underneath,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.040
which gives me a little guide

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.000
to make sure I can line this up properly.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.810
If the tape is not placed carefully,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.250
you're going to alter that line.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.780
It's not going to be a
smooth, straight finish,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.530
and it's not going to look factory.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.460
The type of paint we're
using today is Angelus paint.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.780
It's specifically designed

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.740
for leather and sneakers in general.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.440
It's a very flexible, durable paint

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.040
that we love to use here.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.950
When I'm painting, I try to focus

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.050
on stretching the paint
out as much as possible

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.980
and making that a really thin coat.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.180
If we're using too much paint,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.070
it's going to be too thick

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.260
and it's going to crack
when the customer wears it.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.570
We're also going to get some brushstrokes,

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.450
so we want to make sure
these are very thin coats

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.383
that we're trying to spread out evenly.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.343
On this shoe, you can see that I chose

00:04:33.343 --> 00:04:35.000
not to tape off the upper of the shoe.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.450
That's because of two reasons.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.175
The upper on the shoe is delicate,

00:04:38.175 --> 00:04:39.680
based off the material,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.988
and also, I don't want to get

00:04:40.988 --> 00:04:42.110
any paint on that upper.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.593
So I'd rather trust my eye

00:04:43.593 --> 00:04:45.376
than the tape to stop the paint.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.370
So, once I put on that last coat of black

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:49.600
and that color is looking full,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.200
I can go ahead and remove all the tape,

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.417
but I need to allow this to dry

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:53.317
for five to 10 minutes,

00:04:53.317 --> 00:04:55.043
so the paint is fully cured.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.980
The paint will cure fully
over the next 24 hours,

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.650
so we recommend the
customers don't wear that.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.080
It just needs to wait 24 hours,

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.883
then you can get them back on the streets.

